WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.780
I want to bring on my next guest.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.410
You know him from The Daily Show.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.670
He's got some hilarious
specials on Comedy Central.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.210
He's the first comedian
that I've ever met.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.300
Please welcome Roy Wood Jr.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.210
Hello Moses

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.340
[Moses] Thank you for being here man.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.860
That's my accent. That's
the best I could do.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.393
Hey thank you man.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:22.460
This is crazy where we are.
It's a full circle moment.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.960
You're honestly the first
comedian I've ever met.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.440
It was maybe 2009.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.530
We were making online
YouTube videos together

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.850
and here we are again.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.850
Oh shit, yeah with the homie Dave!

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:38.066
Yeah!

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:41.337
Yeah, okay, okay, yeah I'm
thinking LA, yeah 2000-

00:00:41.337 --> 00:00:44.040
Yeah, its been a minute since then.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.130
Oh my God I'm old.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:46.680
Yeah, it's weird where we are.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.120
We're kind of essentially, every comic is

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.020
becoming a YouTuber.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.970
[Roy] Yeah, that's what I'm learning,

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.450
I'm getting to that place now.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.380
Ordering mics, and super
cool sound proofing equipment

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.660
to help my career. Its like, oh God.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.970
Yeah, a lot of the
soundproofing stuff I'm ordering

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.910
is just so like my girlfriend doesn't hear

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.380
how embarrassing online stand-up is.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.070
[Roy] Yeah, there's something to it

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.040
where even with the emojis coming up,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.660
and people like clapping or whatever,

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.130
I still feel like I'm bombing.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.850
I don't know how you
guys do the online show.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.910
I tried a couple early in quarantine

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.160
and I just knew immediately,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.740
I don't have the performance shot.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.330
It's a special talent to come
through somebodies phone.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.260
Yeah.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.940
[Roy] You got to bring it, and my comedy

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.640
doesn't bring it physically.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.470
It's not expressive. It's just,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.590
I'm the guy sitting in
the corner complaining.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.980
If you pay attention you
might learn something,

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.260
but that doesn't come
through on our phones

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.720
The things that you told me back

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.250
in 2009 are things I
still carry with me today.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.080
Well thank you man, I'm
glad it was decent advice.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.160
I'm glad I wasn't a bitter, angry soul

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.020
who just told you to go get
a job and go to college.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.370
Quit! We don't need another

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.203
white guy talking about Triscuits.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.280
But that's the thing I want to bring up

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.300
is that you wrote an article in Vulture,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.970
essentially of the future of comedy.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.330
And it talked about,
essentially, the lowest man

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.460
on the totem pole of how everyone
kind of moves down a rung.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.384
You were playing arenas, you're not

00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:20.100
going to be playing comedy
clubs, and anyone that was-

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.260
The biggest venues will open last,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.240
so the comics who played
the biggest venues

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.820
will play the venue the
next size down essentially.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.653
Yeah.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.060
Which forces everybody
below them down a rung,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.603
which cuts your money, which means you

00:02:34.603 --> 00:02:37.820
need to figure out
another way to make money.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.940
And I think guys are figuring out, like,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.940
just for perspective,
what's the guy? Terry Fator?

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:48.040
I'm not sure how to pronounce
his last, I think its Fator,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.180
plays in Vegas, has a
regular residency in Vegas.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.200
He's doing, like, comedy clubs right now

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.630
until the Vegas venues open up.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.030
So, you know he's gonna sell tickets,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.380
and the comedy clubs have
to book the best acts,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.090
because they're trying
to keep their lights on.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.240
So, it's nothing, I didn't say that

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.670
in a way to hate on comics.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.430
I'm just saying that's how the industry's

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:10.890
going to have to survive.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:11.723
No, I mean-

00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:13.420
That's what has to happen, and God bless

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.160
the big-wigs for coming back to the clubs

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.170
for a little while to keep the clubs open.

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.890
So, you know, I think it's a good thing.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.380
No, I think a hundred percent.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.810
I mean, if anything it prepared me,

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.720
because I'm exactly the
person you're talking about.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.400
Where I'm the lowest
man on the totem pole,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.860
where you're just headlining,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.110
you're just starting to fill rooms,

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.140
and then everything has
completely come true.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.780
Everything that you wrote
down in the article,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.410
and this is really early
March, when people are like,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.350
this will probably be over in May.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.130
It's all coming true now. So I wonder,

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:42.200
what do you see now?
So now that was, what?

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.090
Four or five months ago. Do you see any,

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.350
is there any future-tellings you have now?

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.860
Here's the thing, like,
everybody thinks about comedy

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.130
as these big name acts coming through,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.250
but like honestly, the
average comedy club,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.350
thirty weeks out of
the year, its road dogs

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.050
that are helping keep the lights on.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.200
It's the Tuesday through Thursday shows.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.950
Those are the shows that help
keep the club profitable.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.750
On the weekends or whatever,
I think that those guys,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.200
I think you have to start
creating your own stages.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.730
You have to start, because
here's what I think,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.590
you gotta bunch of
restaurants that are fucked

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:21.040
because they can't get as many people

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.910
in as they want, or people are scared.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.720
So you gotta have
something to attract them.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.360
So it might be a symbiotic
relationship, you know?

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.690
The think is just that the
bigger issue right now,

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.530
what I didn't feel like I accounted for,

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.330
was the massive amount of unemployment.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:36.496
Yeah

00:04:36.496 --> 00:04:40.740
[Roy] As entertainers, we are
disposable income activity.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.670
So, even if the clubs are opening back up,

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.410
how many people have
twenty to thirty dollars

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.110
to spend? And that's not counting
all the people surviving.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.780
Everybody counts Covid in deaths,

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.450
but the people who survive, bitch,

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.390
they've got medical bills out their ass.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:55.223
Right

00:04:55.223 --> 00:04:56.850
[Roy] How many people are going to be able

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.060
to go out and just have a night out?

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.450
Like on a date night in a comedy club,

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.370
that's an eighty dollar spend.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.600
Twenty on tickets, twenty
on drinks, it's forty ahead.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.580
As someone who hates this,
and has very best interest,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.200
it is a waste of money to be in a club.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.330
Especially when there are so
many great Netflix specials,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:17.270
people on Twitch, people on
YouTube doing the same thing

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.800
or something higher quality
that you could stay home.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.900
So why go out and spend
money on two drinks,

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:25.770
a whole plate of food, risk your life,

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.180
and then the odd chance
that the comedian is good.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.440
Do you think that, so Chris Rock

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.527
had a quote years ago where he said that

00:05:33.527 --> 00:05:37.003
"everything that I do is to
bring people to my live show"

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.200
anything that he did within his portfolio

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.710
was to sell a seventy or eighty,

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.100
whatever his ticket cost is to
get someone to see him live.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.700
Do you think it's the flip now?

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.360
Where if you do the live show for free,

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.920
you do the live show at a cheaper rate,

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.850
you do it at a park, or a drive-in,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:54.550
or whatever wacky place the next year

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.570
holds for us at thirty percent capacity.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.850
Do you think the live
show is the new conduit

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.470
to drive people online where you can

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.090
monetize through advertisements.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.820
Via your podcast, if
you have a web series,

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.220
or whatever the fuck else you're doing,

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:11.889
I honestly feel like-

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:12.722
[Moses] I think you're looking ahead.

00:06:12.722 --> 00:06:14.420
Yes, I do think yes that is,

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:15.620
but I think that a lot of comedians

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.020
have not understood that yet.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.360
I think a lot of them are very foolish.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.830
I think there's a lot of
people within our craft

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.500
that are very dismissive of technology.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.700
They're very dismissive of the evolution

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.640
of how people consume information.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.010
And at the end of the day,
you can be the purest,

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.680
I start on the stage, I'm old school.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.060
Okay, but you gotta go
where the people are

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.780
and how people are taking in stuff.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.430
I honestly think that Chappelle

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.390
has changed how the hour
special will be constructed.

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:49.080
If you look at the turn time from, shoot,

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.710
like, the average hour
special is probably shot

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.713
eight months before it airs, right?

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.810
And that window is getting
shorter and shorter

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.600
because public attention
spans are shorter,

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.500
the news cycle changed, if you write

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.620
a joke in April, that's not even relevant

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.670
in fucking August, by the time it airs.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.300
[Moses] Yeah.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.140
Yvonne Orji I think had a,
she shot her HBO special

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:17.690
in February, and I think
it aired Mother's Day,

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:21.030
so that's maybe three
months from shoot to air.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.950
That's an incredibly short runway.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:26.873
And then Chappelle did
836 which, I would argue,

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.790
two months total, working
on some of that material.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:32.690
But so much of it was about George Floyd,

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:37.190
and so close to it,
above maybe three weeks

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.560
from conception to air.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.112
[Moses] So does that mean that

00:07:40.112 --> 00:07:40.945
the special gets less special,

00:07:40.945 --> 00:07:42.160
and people are not putting ten years

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.000
of work into a special?
Like I was just about

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.750
to tape my special right
before the shut down,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.410
and that's like ten years of work.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.006
So now are you looking at just a comic

00:07:50.006 --> 00:07:51.050
that's been on the road for six months?

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.620
That's the special, comes out immediately.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:54.920
I think that you have to tap into,

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.850
right now, people are more connected

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.920
to emotions than laughs,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.500
and I think Chappelle's shit worked

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.290
because he touched peoples emotions.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:05.650
And so, I don't think it makes a special

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.790
any less special, it just has to be

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.070
more in the moment, and right now,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:13.360
our moments change faster than they did

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.360
under the previous comedy
construction timeline.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.500
The assembly line has to be shorter now.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.960
So, you can still have an hour special,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.860
but if you had a special, I
give you a perfect example,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.620
so I was like forty percent,
I had about forty percent

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.946
of my next hour done when Covid hit

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.910
with the plan of taping in November.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:40.150
So, around March I had about
twenty, twenty-five minutes

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:41.422
that I went-

00:08:41.422 --> 00:08:42.255
[Hands Clapping]

00:08:42.255 --> 00:08:44.713
this is good, this is
for sure, we'll work.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.650
There's probably four or
five minutes of that material

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:52.750
that I would even want to spew
now because just tonally, no.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.640
Like I had a whole thing about

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.440
Congressional hearings, and how bullshit

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.340
the concept of a Congressional hearing,

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.710
nobody wants to hear about that.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.710
Good luck getting anyone to
care about any of that stuff.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.090
But in January,

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:05.923
Yeah.

00:09:05.923 --> 00:09:07.940
That was the thing, and
it was always the thing,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.530
and it happened, its crazy to think

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.430
that I have jokes about mass shootings,

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:15.480
and the whole concept of
how and where they occur,

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.000
and how the coverage is,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.540
how the media covers mass shootings.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.533
Even that is eh.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:23.223
[Moses] Yeah.

00:09:23.223 --> 00:09:25.190
Do you even want to talk
about that right now?

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.780
Because even when a mass shooting happens,

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.320
it doesn't get the coverage
that it got pre-Covid.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.250
Because there's just
something more ominous,

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:32.640
and then you don't know who the fuck

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.060
is gonna win in November.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.120
Which is also gonna change
the tone of the country, so.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.470
You do a lot of political humor,

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:39.357
how do you feel about that?

00:09:39.357 --> 00:09:41.300
How do you feel about
doing another four years

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.600
of essentially Trump jokes.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.360
I mean with The Daily
Show, it's all encouraged,

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.500
it's what you cover. So where
do you feel like that is?

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.250
If Trump wins again, do you
have it in you to just be like,

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:53.850
what else could you make fun of?

00:09:55.214 --> 00:09:58.090
I don't know if you can
make fun of it anymore

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.300
because it just keeps getting
more and more serious.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.960
It's like the vice just keeps getting

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.470
tighter, and tighter, tighter.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:05.890
I think the last time I
righteously laughed at Trump

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:07.973
was when he looked at the solar eclipse.

00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:10.470
[Both Laugh]

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:13.740
Yeah, the time he looked
into the sun, yeah.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.170
Yeah and like the day
after that he's like,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:17.834
all right, get them kids in the cages,

00:10:17.834 --> 00:10:21.001
and I was like, I can't laugh anymore.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:26.560
I don't know, man. I know
that at the end of the day,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.210
it's my job as a corespondent to put

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:30.430
a face to the people that are struggling

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:32.400
and dealing with a lot
of b.s. in the country,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.470
and I find a joke in that.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.050
So, I'll have to keep doing that, but,

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.499
you know, you take a deep
breath and do your job.

00:10:38.499 --> 00:10:42.380
I would prefer that
that's not the situation

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.020
come Wednesday morning in November,

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:45.690
but, you know, we'll see.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.640
Are there things that maybe
comics should be doing.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.350
So obviously investing in themselves.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.973
You know, things they should not be doing.

00:10:51.973 --> 00:10:53.010
Have you seen things of like, oof,

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:56.350
don't go live everyday, no one
needs to hear your thoughts.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:57.800
I wouldn't say don't go live everyday,

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.690
